WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.031 --> 00:00:01.633
There are a few signs that it might

2
00:00:01.673 --> 00:00:03.916
be time to move beyond a browser-based

3
00:00:03.976 --> 00:00:04.297
builder.

4
00:00:05.118 --> 00:00:07.461
First, some users report that these

5
00:00:07.521 --> 00:00:09.544
builders may not retain context

6
00:00:09.644 --> 00:00:10.225
effectively.

7
00:00:10.926 --> 00:00:12.648
You might find yourself describing the

8
00:00:12.708 --> 00:00:14.971
same project details over and over.

9
00:00:15.572 --> 00:00:17.595
Second, you may want to use a specific

10
00:00:17.675 --> 00:00:20.899
library or an unusual integration that the

11
00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:23.182
builder doesn’t support.

12
00:00:23.182 --> 00:00:25.527
Lastly, if you need to collaborate with

13
00:00:25.568 --> 00:00:27.713
someone else, that can be tough in a

14
00:00:27.813 --> 00:00:28.875
browser environment.

15
00:00:29.457 --> 00:00:31.341
None of these points are about difficulty.

16
00:00:31.922 --> 00:00:33.887
They’re more about ownership and control

17
00:00:33.927 --> 00:00:35.230
over your project.

18
00:00:35.953 --> 00:00:38.255
When you transition, your project moves

19
00:00:38.315 --> 00:00:40.618
from being inside a builder to existing as

20
00:00:40.638 --> 00:00:42.380
actual files on your computer.

21
00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:44.942
This change gives you more control.

22
00:00:45.663 --> 00:00:48.165
You can store those files safely, share

23
00:00:48.205 --> 00:00:50.708
them with a developer, or even switch to

24
00:00:50.728 --> 00:00:52.069
a different tool if you want.

25
00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:54.232
None of that is possible when you’re

26
00:00:54.272 --> 00:00:56.133
working within a browser-based builder.

27
00:00:56.814 --> 00:00:57.975
The trade-off?

28
00:00:58.375 --> 00:01:00.377
You’ll need to deal with some setup —

29
00:01:00.377 --> 00:01:03.220
things like installations, a terminal, and

30
00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:04.922
a few new terms to learn.

31
00:01:05.543 --> 00:01:07.985
Now, let's talk about what an AI coding

32
00:01:08.025 --> 00:01:09.267
agent actually does.

33
00:01:10.007 --> 00:01:12.209
It’s not just autocomplete or a chat

34
00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:13.571
window that knows code.

35
00:01:14.171 --> 00:01:15.973
When you give it a request, it looks

36
00:01:16.033 --> 00:01:17.535
through your project for relevant

37
00:01:17.575 --> 00:01:18.155
information.

38
00:01:18.756 --> 00:01:21.138
Then, it decides which files to change,

39
00:01:21.539 --> 00:01:23.741
makes those edits, and can even run the

40
00:01:23.781 --> 00:01:25.763
project to check if everything works.

41
00:01:26.543 --> 00:01:28.085
That last part is crucial.

42
00:01:28.705 --> 00:01:31.208
It verifies its own work, which can save

43
00:01:31.248 --> 00:01:33.390
you a lot of headaches down the line.

44
00:01:34.045 --> 00:01:35.887
Next, let’s see this in action.

45
00:01:36.547 --> 00:01:38.809
What follows is a genuine recording of an

46
00:01:38.989 --> 00:01:41.392
AI coding agent at work.

47
00:01:41.792 --> 00:01:43.613
Nothing is staged, and you’ll see

48
00:01:43.793 --> 00:01:44.974
everything as it happens.

49
00:01:45.615 --> 00:01:47.437
We’ll use the habit tracker project from

50
00:01:47.477 --> 00:01:49.739
lesson three, which has been exported to

51
00:01:49.799 --> 00:01:50.179
files.

52
00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:52.661
The request will be to add a weekly

53
00:01:52.761 --> 00:01:55.144
summary feature, which touches multiple

54
00:01:55.204 --> 00:01:56.184
parts of the project.

55
00:01:56.825 --> 00:01:58.567
This is where a builder might struggle,

56
00:01:58.847 --> 00:02:00.368
but an agent can really help.

57
00:02:01.089 --> 00:02:02.390
Watch what it does.

58
00:02:02.944 --> 00:02:05.547
After the agent completes its task, it

59
00:02:05.587 --> 00:02:07.470
looks at the project before making any

60
00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:08.010
changes.

61
00:02:08.851 --> 00:02:10.574
That’s why it can place new code in

62
00:02:10.614 --> 00:02:12.917
the right spot instead of creating a new

63
00:02:12.977 --> 00:02:13.297
file.

64
00:02:13.818 --> 00:02:15.840
It often changes more than one file

65
00:02:16.161 --> 00:02:18.564
because features usually involve multiple

66
00:02:18.624 --> 00:02:19.605
parts of a project.

67
00:02:20.226 --> 00:02:22.549
Plus, it provides a summary of what it

68
00:02:22.609 --> 00:02:22.809
did.

69
00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:24.812
Make sure to read that part.

70
00:02:25.372 --> 00:02:27.775
It’s your only record of what changed if

71
00:02:27.816 --> 00:02:29.778
you don’t check the files yourself.

72
00:02:30.265 --> 00:02:32.628
To keep your project safe, start using

73
00:02:32.708 --> 00:02:34.291
version control from day one.

74
00:02:35.132 --> 00:02:36.914
This is something we’ll cover in lesson

75
00:02:36.974 --> 00:02:39.197
nine, and it’s what makes features like

76
00:02:39.638 --> 00:02:41.621
'undo everything since Tuesday' possible.

77
00:02:42.181 --> 00:02:44.304
Also, take a moment to read the summary

78
00:02:44.344 --> 00:02:46.848
of changes, even if you don’t dive into

79
00:02:46.888 --> 00:02:47.308
the code.

80
00:02:48.069 --> 00:02:49.131
If there’s something you don’t

81
00:02:49.171 --> 00:02:51.374
understand, ask the agent for an

82
00:02:51.474 --> 00:02:52.175
explanation.

83
00:02:52.575 --> 00:02:55.239
There are no stupid questions here, and

84
00:02:55.259 --> 00:02:57.642
the explanation is part of the process.

85
00:02:58.398 --> 00:03:00.424
In the next lesson, we’ll focus on

86
00:03:00.484 --> 00:03:00.925
design.

87
00:03:01.727 --> 00:03:03.733
This step will help you bring your ideas

88
00:03:03.793 --> 00:03:06.059
to fruition and make your project look

89
00:03:06.099 --> 00:03:07.924
like someone carefully chose its

90
00:03:07.965 --> 00:03:08.847
appearance.

